One of my citizens' band radio handles was NaySayer, so take
all this with the warning it comes from a pessimist, but
still...

You have some real interface issues; yes, orthogonal motion
only goes from four keys to six keys, but diagonal motion
goes from four keys to 20 keys, and you've used up most of
(one case of) common alphabets, or pretty much everything if
you want <shift>-char to mean "move 'char' direction a lot".

You have some really interesting choices about PC and NPC
motion:

1) If everything can fly, the solid parts don't need to be
particularly well connected for motion to be possible, just
the void parts, but for the void parts "connected at a
vertex, or at an edge" is ridiculous for PCs and NPCs with
"thickness", so you have to do better than that.  Can you
"hang stuff in space" or does having an object in a cell
require that cell to have one solid face?

2) If everything is pretty much gravityless, like ants, say,
but needs a gripping surface to move, then the solid parts
also have to be connected, and whether you can step across
vertex or edge connected solid bits is an interesting call,
but at least you needn't pay attention to how hard it is to
move each direction or how long it takes.

3) If everything is massive like humans, going horizontal on
up pointing surfaces is easy, going horizontal on down
pointing surfaces is a worst case, going vertical _up_ is
slower and consumes more food energy that going vertical
down, rappelling down is faster, probably, than running
horizontally, some directions of motion take special
supplies like ropes and clips and spikes and hammers; it
gets very complicated just to discuss, much less to
implement, very quickly.

4) Humans descended from the trees a very long time ago,
we don't think as well in 3D as, say, a gibbon or a lemure,
so playing at all is going to be an intense intellectual
effort for us as players, not as PCs, and might not be all
that attractive.

5) If you completely choose to ignore real life
considerations like this, you'll be derided constantly.

You have a similar set of problems with objects and dungeon
dressing and such; you need extra commands to pick something
down off a ceiling, and very likely you cannot do that while
standing on the floor, unless you play for the Dungeon
Crawlers on the National Basketball Association.  Moreover,
presumably something on a ceiling or a wall cannot just be
dropped back there when you are done with it, you broke the
silk spider web or Crazy Glue or whatever was holding it in
place; even if it was in a clip, that's a lot slower to
return to its previous condition than if it were laying on
the floor.  Gravity is going to be a constant headache.
Accounting for time is going to be even more a headache
than in NetHack now.

Got a pool?  Are you sure? It doesn't leak out the bottom
somewhere?

Ricochettes are probably solvable; visibility is going to
burn CPU cycles like mad.

Does crossing from solid part to solid part at a vertex
require a dexterity check?

Do monsters advertise their approach by shadows cast ahead
of them?

Where, exactly, is the light source that makes that work? Is
the effect noticable compared to light on the same surface
from other light sources?

You'd have _lots_ of fun doing this, but you'd also find out
very quickly why it would have been a better call to go from
2D to 1D if you wanted to change dimensionality; now you
only have to list what can and cannot pass what in a tunnel,
and you have keyboard keys to spare.

